---
/**
 * The core loop, drawn rather than screenshotted.
 *
 * WHY THIS REPLACED A SCREENSHOT. The hero used to carry a crop of the
 * quoted-vs-today panel. It proved one narrow claim (a confirmed quote holds
 * its price) in the largest slot on the page, while the thing a first-time
 * reader actually cannot work out is what the product IS: that the whole job
 * is one chain, and the end of it feeds the front. A cropped panel cannot say
 * that. This can, in one glance, which is what a hero slot is for.
 *
 * WHY THESE SIX STOPS AND NOT src/lib/workflow.ts's FOURTEEN. The fourteen are
 * the work in the order it happens and they are the right grain for the loop
 * SECTION, where each one gets a screen beside it. Fourteen labels in a hero
 * band is a bus map. These six are a condensation of that same order, never a
 * different chain:
 *
 *   PRICE IN                    -> workflow.ts steps 1-2  (PRICE IN, THE DIFFS)
 *   COST IT                     -> steps 3-5  (THE TARGET, THE PLATE, THE MENU)
 *   ORDER                       -> steps 6-7  (THE ORDER, THE PRICE)
 *   SHOP                        -> step 9     (SHOP)
 *   PREP                        -> step 8     (THE PANS)
 *   PACK                        -> the pack plan
 *   return: RECEIVE             -> steps 10-13 (PO OUT ... ON HAND)
 *
 * If workflow.ts's order ever changes, this changes with it. Two drawings of
 * one chain that disagree is worse than no drawing.
 *
 * SHOP / PREP / PACK are set in the mono micro-label voice on purpose: that
 * trio is the brand's own repeated device, and these are the words a caterer
 * already uses for the sheets they hold.
 *
 * NOT AUTOMATION, CONNECTION. The return path says what you paid becomes the
 * next price. It does not say the software buys, keys, or decides anything:
 * invoices are reviewed, extracted values are proposals, and price changes are
 * never silently applied. See the forbidden-claims list in
 * scripts/check-landing-claims.mjs for why that distinction is guarded.
 */
const stops = [
	{ tag: 'PRICE IN', label: 'A vendor price sheet or invoice' },
	{ tag: 'COST IT', label: 'Recipes and menus cost themselves' },
	{ tag: 'ORDER', label: 'A menu plus a head count' },
	{ tag: 'SHOP', label: 'By supplier, in whole packs' },
	{ tag: 'PREP', label: 'Scaled batches, whole pots' },
	{ tag: 'PACK', label: 'Portions, equipment, notes' }
];
---

<figure class="mt-12 lg:mt-16">
	<div class="border-y border-ticket-rule bg-cream px-5 py-8 sm:px-8 lg:px-10 lg:py-10">
		<!-- The diagram is decorative in its arrows only; the chain itself is a
		     real ordered list, so a screen reader gets the sequence rather than a
		     wall of disconnected labels. -->
		<p class="sr-only">
			The CostCook loop, in order: a vendor price sheet or invoice comes in, recipes and
			menus cost themselves, a menu plus a head count becomes an order, the order becomes a
			shopping list by supplier in whole packs, then scaled prep batches, then the pack
			plan. Receiving what you bought returns to the start, so the price you actually paid
			becomes the price the next quote is costed from.
		</p>

		<ol
			role="list"
			class="grid grid-cols-[repeat(auto-fit,minmax(8rem,1fr))] gap-x-5 gap-y-7 sm:grid-cols-3 lg:grid-cols-6 lg:gap-x-7"
			aria-hidden="true"
		>
			{
				stops.map((stop, i) => (
					<li class="relative">
						{/* The connector is drawn on the item that FOLLOWS the gap, so the
						    last stop never trails an arrow into empty space. It is hidden
						    wherever the grid wraps (2- and 3-column), because a horizontal
						    arrow across a line break points at the wrong neighbour.

						    A glyph, not a hairline. The first cut drew a 12px dashed rule
						    here and at label size it read as a hyphen stuck to the front of
						    the word: "---COST IT". An arrow cannot be misread as
						    punctuation. */}
						{i > 0 && (
							<span
								class="pointer-events-none absolute -top-px -left-[1.35rem] hidden text-sm leading-none text-ink-soft/55 lg:block"
								aria-hidden="true"
							>
								&#8594;
							</span>
						)}
						<p class="font-mono text-eyebrow font-semibold tracking-wide text-amber-deep">
							{stop.tag}
						</p>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-snug text-ink-soft">{stop.label}</p>
					</li>
				))
			}
		</ol>

		<!-- The return path. On large screens it reads as a line coming back
		     under the chain; below that the arrow glyph would point across a
		     wrapped grid, so it degrades to the same sentence without the rule. -->
		<div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 border-t border-dashed border-ink-rule/45 pt-5">
			<span
				class="font-mono text-eyebrow font-semibold tracking-wide whitespace-nowrap text-ink-soft"
			>
				<span aria-hidden="true">&#8629;</span> RECEIVE
			</span>
			<p class="text-sm leading-snug text-ink-soft">
				Tick off what actually arrived, and the price you paid becomes the price the next
				quote is costed from. You approve it; nothing changes behind your back.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
	<figcaption class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink-soft">
		<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">One chain, and it closes.</strong> Every step is a
		screen you already work in. The numbers move between them so you do not.
	</figcaption>
</figure>
